# Goal 9: Fix pow — widespread computation errors

## Function
`pow` (double precision, aliased from `powd`)

## Source Files
- C source: `/home/leochanj/Desktop/libmcs/libm/mathd/powd.c`
- Rust source: `/home/leochanj/Desktop/libmcs/newexp/rust-s6/src/mathd.rs` (line ~3504)

## Problem
MISMATCH — 20 test failures (the most of any function).

The Rust `pow` produces wrong results across many inputs, including trivial cases:

Examples:
- C: `pow(2, 10) = 0x1p+10` (1024) → Rust: `0x1.b2d809254afbcp+11` (~3493)
- C: `pow(2, 1000) = 0x1p+1000` → Rust: `0x1.ffffffffbbp+999` (off by ~1 ULP but wrong)
- C: `pow(2, -1000) = 0x1p-1000` → Rust: `0x1.ffffffffbb4p-1001` (wrong exponent)

Even `pow(2, 10)` is wildly wrong, suggesting a fundamental algorithm bug, not just precision.

## What Needs to Change
The core `powd` algorithm has a major bug. Compare against the C `powd.c` implementation. Likely issues:
- Incorrect log2 decomposition or reconstruction
- Wrong polynomial coefficients
- Missing or incorrect scaling step
- Error in the two-part multiplication (hi/lo arithmetic)

This needs careful line-by-line comparison with the C source.

## Success Criteria
- `pow(2, 10)` returns exactly `0x1p+10` (1024.0).
- `pow(2, 1000)` returns exactly `0x1p+1000`.
- `pow(2, -1000)` returns exactly `0x1p-1000`.
- All 20 pow mismatches resolve to bitwise-exact match with C output.
